sections.s2_technical:
ECS is AWS's own orchestrator. You define a task definition — the container image, CPU, memory, environment, and an IAM task role — and a service that keeps a desired number of tasks running behind a load balancer. The concepts are few: clusters, services, tasks, and task definitions. Because AWS built it, it integrates cleanly with IAM, CloudWatch, ALB, VPC networking, and ECR, and there is no control plane for you to run or pay for. The trade-off is that ECS is AWS-specific; you cannot lift an ECS service unchanged to another cloud or on-premises.

```java
// An ECS service idea: desired count of a task behind a load balancer
record EcsService(String name, String taskDefinition, int desiredCount, String launchType) {}
EcsService transfer = new EcsService("transfer-service",
    "transfer-task:1.4.0", 3, "FARGATE");
```

EKS is managed Kubernetes. AWS runs and patches the Kubernetes control plane, and you run workloads as pods described by Kubernetes manifests, using the full Kubernetes API, Helm charts, operators, and the enormous cloud-native ecosystem. The big advantage is portability and ecosystem: the same manifests can run on EKS, on another cloud's managed Kubernetes, or on-premises, so you avoid lock-in and reuse Kubernetes tooling. The cost is complexity — you must understand Kubernetes concepts like deployments, services, ingress, namespaces, and config maps, manage cluster upgrades and add-ons, and usually staff engineers who know Kubernetes.

```java
// A Kubernetes deployment idea expressed as simple config for clarity
record K8sDeployment(String name, String image, int replicas, String namespace) {}
K8sDeployment transfer = new K8sDeployment("transfer-service",
    "transfer:1.4.0", 3, "payments");
```

Fargate is the compute layer that removes servers from the picture for both ECS and EKS. Normally your containers run on EC2 instances that you provision, patch, scale, and secure. With Fargate you stop managing instances entirely: you declare the CPU and memory each task (ECS) or pod (EKS) needs, and AWS runs it on infrastructure it manages and bills you per task for the resources used. This removes a whole category of operational work — no instance patching, no cluster capacity planning in the same way — at a per-unit price premium over self-managed EC2. The alternative launch type is EC2, where you run the containers on your own instances for more control and potentially lower cost at high steady utilization.

A senior nuance: the choice between ECS and EKS is usually organizational, not technical. If a team is all-in on AWS and values simplicity and speed, ECS is the pragmatic choice with less to learn and operate. If an organization needs multi-cloud portability, already has deep Kubernetes investment, or wants the Kubernetes ecosystem, EKS is worth its complexity. Fargate is orthogonal — it is about whether you want to manage servers, and I default to Fargate to shed operational burden, dropping to EC2 launch type only when I need specific instance features, GPUs, or cost efficiency at high sustained scale.

